values.value:
== LWC — LIGHTNING WEB COMPONENT GENERATION ==

== FILE STRUCTURE ==
Every LWC requires EXACTLY THREE file entries in files[]:

  1. metadataType: LwcHtml   filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.html     → HTML template source
  2. metadataType: LwcJs     filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.js       → JavaScript source
  3. metadataType: LwcMeta   filePath: lwc/{name}/{name}.js-meta.xml → XML only

The field in the JSON response for all three is "xmlContent" (consistent with all other types).
NEVER put <?xml into a .html or .js file.
NEVER put <template> or import statements into a .js-meta.xml file.

Component naming rules:
- DeveloperName (memberName): camelCase, starts lowercase, no spaces (e.g. accountCard)
- If ticket says "Account Card" → memberName = accountCard
- If ticket says "AccountCard" → memberName = accountCard (lowercase first letter)

== JS-META.XML FORMAT ==
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<LightningComponentBundle xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <apiVersion>61.0</apiVersion>
    <isExposed>true</isExposed>
    <targets>
        <target>lightning__RecordPage</target>
        <target>lightning__AppPage</target>
    </targets>
</LightningComponentBundle>

Target selection rules:
- Default (ticket does not specify): lightning__RecordPage + lightning__AppPage
- "Record page" or "on the account/contact/case record": lightning__RecordPage only
- "App page" or "home page" or "utility bar": use the matching target
- "Flow screen": lightning__FlowScreen only
- NEVER include <targetConfigs> UNLESS the component has an @api property
  that must be configurable by an admin placing it directly on a page via
  Lightning App Builder (design time) — see the required exception below.
  For every other component, keep meta simple and omit it.

-- EXCEPTION: <targetConfigs> FOR AN ADMIN-CONFIGURABLE @api PROPERTY --
A component placed directly on a page (not embedded as a child of another
LWC that passes it props programmatically) has exactly ONE way for an
admin to set a non-recordId @api property's value: a design-time property
declared in <targetConfigs>, which then appears as an input in the App
Builder properties panel when the component is selected. Skipping
<targetConfigs> for a ticket that requires this (e.g. "accepts a
configurable list of field API names as a public property") leaves that
@api property permanently stuck at its default — there is no other path
for a value to ever reach it, and any wire/logic gated on it will never
receive real data.

Design-time properties in <targetConfigs> can ONLY be scalar — String,
Boolean, or Integer — never an array or object. Represent a list-shaped
@api property as a comma-separated String design property instead, and
have the component's @api accessor split it into an array internally:

  <targetConfigs>
      <targetConfig targets="lightning__RecordPage">
          <property name="fieldApiNames" type="String"
              label="Field API Names (comma-separated, object-qualified — e.g. Account.Name,Account.Industry)"
              default="Account.Name,Account.Industry" />
      </targetConfig>
  </targetConfigs>

  // In the .js — accept the CSV string and expose it as an array to the
  // rest of the component via a getter/setter pair, not a plain field:
  _fieldApiNames = [];

  @api
  get fieldApiNames() {
      return this._fieldApiNames;
  }
  set fieldApiNames(value) {
      this._fieldApiNames = typeof value === 'string'
          ? value.split(',').map((f) => f.trim()).filter((f) => f)
          : (Array.isArray(value) ? value : []);
  }

This accepts BOTH shapes — a CSV string from App Builder's design property,
and a plain array if the same component is ever also used programmatically
as a child of another LWC — so it is not locked to one placement style.
Always give the design property a non-empty `default` so the component
renders something meaningful immediately after being dropped on a page,
before an admin has customized it.

== DEPLOYMENT PLAN ==
LWC-only deploys always use scenario = "single".
If the ticket also creates a new CustomObject: object in ZIP1, layout in ZIP2, LWC always in ZIP1.

== MERGE MODE vs CREATE MODE ==
The decision is made ENTIRELY from org state — ticket wording does NOT override it.

STEP ZERO — CHECK orgState.existingLwcComponent BEFORE WRITING ANYTHING.

  IF orgState.existingLwcComponent IS NOT NULL → MERGE MODE (see below)
  IF orgState.existingLwcComponent IS NULL     → CREATE MODE (see below)

Words like "create an LWC", "build a component", "add an LWC" describe BUSINESS INTENT.
If existingLwcComponent is not null, you are ALWAYS in MERGE MODE regardless of ticket wording.

-- MERGE MODE — existingLwcComponent IS NOT NULL --
STEP 1: List every section and element in existingLwcComponent.html. ALL must appear in output.
STEP 2: List every import, property (@api, @track, @wire), and method in existingLwcComponent.js. ALL must appear in output.
STEP 3: Identify what the ticket is adding or changing.
STEP 4: Start from existingLwcComponent.html verbatim. Apply only the requested change.
STEP 5: Start from existingLwcComponent.js verbatim. Apply only the requested change.
STEP 6: SELF-CHECK:
  - Does output HTML contain every section from STEP 1? If not, add missing ones.
  - Does output JS contain every import and method from STEP 2? If not, paste them back.
  - Is output the COMPLETE file — not a snippet?
STEP 7: Emit all three files (html, js, meta). The meta file may be unchanged — still include it.

-- CREATE MODE — existingLwcComponent IS NULL --
Generate all three files from scratch.
Use the component name from orgState or infer from ticket context.

== TIER 1 — WIRE-BASED (no Apex, read-only display) ==
Use when: ticket asks to display record fields, related list, or object data.
No Apex class needed.

-- TEMPLATE PATTERN (Tier 1) --
<template>
    <lightning-card title="Card Title" icon-name="standard:account">
        <div class="slds-p-around_medium">
            <template if:true={record.data}>
                <p><strong>Name:</strong> {name}</p>
                <p><strong>Industry:</strong> {industry}</p>
            </template>
            <template if:true={record.error}>
                <p class="slds-text-color_error">Error loading data.</p>
            </template>
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 1, getRecord wire) --
import { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';
import { getRecord } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';
import NAME_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Name';
import INDUSTRY_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Industry';

export default class AccountCard extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;

    @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: [NAME_FIELD, INDUSTRY_FIELD] })
    record;

    get name() {
        return this.record.data?.fields?.Name?.value;
    }

    get industry() {
        return this.record.data?.fields?.Industry?.value;
    }
}

TIER 1 RULES:
- Use @api recordId when the component lives on a record page
- Import each field separately using @salesforce/schema/ObjectName.FieldName
- Use optional chaining (?.) when reading wire data fields
- Use if:true={record.data} and if:true={record.error} — ALWAYS both, never one without the other
- Never hardcode record IDs

== TIER 2 — APEX-CONNECTED (custom SOQL, DML, or business logic) ==
Use when: ticket asks to display or change data that requires custom SOQL,
DML, or business logic. An Apex controller MUST be generated or read.

Tier 2 has TWO sub-patterns — TIER 2A (wired) and TIER 2B (imperative). A
single component can use BOTH for different operations — apply the rule per
operation, not once for the whole component.

== TIER 2 DECISION RULE — WIRED vs IMPERATIVE APEX ==
Use TIER 2A — @wire — when ALL are true for that operation: it only READS
data (never DML); it should load automatically on mount or re-run when a
reactive @api parameter changes; the user doesn't need to control exactly
when it fires (no button/form gating the fetch).

Use TIER 2B — imperative (.then().catch()) — when ANY are true: it performs
DML (insert/update/delete/upsert) or any write; it must fire on a specific
user action (click, submit, row selection) rather than automatically; it
needs parameters gathered from user input at the moment of the action.

A wired result can still support "Refresh" without switching to imperative —
use refreshApex(wiredResult) to force a cacheable result to refetch. Never
abandon @wire just because a Refresh button exists.

If genuinely ambiguous: default to imperative — always safe, just less
idiomatic than @wire for a pure read-only, load-time case.

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 2A, @wire PROPERTY form) --
import { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';
import { refreshApex } from '@salesforce/apex';
import getInvoices from '@salesforce/apex/InvoiceController.getInvoices';

export default class InvoiceList extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;

    @wire(getInvoices, { accountId: '$recordId' })
    wiredInvoices;

    get invoices() {
        return this.wiredInvoices.data;
    }

    get error() {
        return this.wiredInvoices.error ? getErrorMessage(this.wiredInvoices.error) : undefined;
    }

    get hasInvoices() {
        return this.invoices && this.invoices.length > 0;
    }

    get isLoading() {
        return !this.wiredInvoices.data && !this.wiredInvoices.error;
    }

    handleRefresh() {
        refreshApex(this.wiredInvoices);
    }
}

Template reuses the same 3-state shape as Tier 2B (spinner/error/data) below
— only the JS source of isLoading/error/data differs.

An alternative FUNCTION-HANDLER form exists — @wire(getX) methodName({ data,
error }) { ... } — for when you need to transform data before storing it. If
used, still keep the raw { data, error } result in its own property so
refreshApex() has something to refetch; never discard it after deriving from it.

TIER 2A RULES:
- A wired Apex adapter's return value is ALWAYS a { data, error } object,
  never a plain array — bind {property}.data / {property}.error in BOTH JS
  and the template. Never bind the bare property alone.
- refreshApex(wiredResult) imports from '@salesforce/apex' (NOT 'lwc') and
  takes the WIRED RESULT object itself — never a derived copy.
- Never assign directly to a wired property's .data — call refreshApex().
- Reactive wire parameters use '$' ({ accountId: '$recordId' }) — the wire
  re-runs automatically when that reactive property changes.
- The Apex method behind a Tier 2A wire MUST be @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true).
- ALWAYS show a loading state until the wire settles — never skip the
  spinner just because @wire fires automatically.

-- TEMPLATE PATTERN (Tier 2B, imperative) --
<template>
    <lightning-card title="Invoice List" icon-name="standard:invoice">
        <div class="slds-p-around_medium">
            <template if:true={isLoading}>
                <lightning-spinner alternative-text="Loading" size="small"></lightning-spinner>
            </template>
            <template if:true={error}>
                <p class="slds-text-color_error">{error}</p>
            </template>
            <template if:true={hasInvoices}>
                <template for:each={invoices} for:item="inv">
                    <p key={inv.Id}>{inv.Name} — {inv.Amount__c}</p>
                </template>
            </template>
        </div>
    </lightning-card>
</template>

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 2B, imperative) --
// CORRECT Tier 2B import — no wire:
import { LightningElement, api, track } from 'lwc';
import getInvoices from '@salesforce/apex/InvoiceController.getInvoices';

export default class InvoiceList extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;
    @track invoices;
    @track isLoading = false;
    @track error;

    get hasInvoices() {
        return this.invoices && this.invoices.length > 0;
    }

    connectedCallback() {
        this.loadData();
    }

    loadData() {
        this.isLoading = true;
        this.error = undefined;
        getInvoices({ accountId: this.recordId })
            .then(result => {
                this.invoices = result;
                this.isLoading = false;
            })
            .catch(err => {
                this.error = getErrorMessage(err);
                this.isLoading = false;
            });
    }

    // Refresh / action button handler pattern:
    // ALWAYS clear data and error first so spinner appears and user sees visual feedback.
    // ALWAYS guard with !this.isLoading to prevent double-calls.
    handleRefresh() {
        if (!this.isLoading) {
            this.invoices = undefined;
            this.error = undefined;
            this.loadData();
        }
    }
}

TIER 2B RULES:
- ALWAYS use @track for properties that drive template re-render
- ALWAYS show lightning-spinner when isLoading is true
- ALWAYS handle the .catch() — never leave it out
- ALWAYS reset error to undefined at the start of loadData()
- ALWAYS call loadData() from connectedCallback() for initial load
- Import path: @salesforce/apex/ClassName.methodName
- Route every caught error through getErrorMessage(err) for display — see
  the getErrorMessage helper and its rules under LWC_NAVIGATION's
  "ERROR MESSAGE EXTRACTION" section. Never read err.body?.message or
  err.message directly; the real failure reason (a validation rule, a
  delete blocked by related records, etc.) is frequently nested deeper
  than the top-level message.
- When a ticket asks for a Refresh/Reload button ON AN IMPERATIVE (Tier 2B) component:
  ALWAYS clear the data property and error to undefined BEFORE calling loadData() — this
  gives visible feedback to the user. Without clearing, the list stays visible during
  reload and the button appears broken.
- Cacheable-ness must match HOW the method is called, not just what it does:
  - Called via @wire (Tier 2A): use @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) — REQUIRED, this is what
    enables refreshApex() to force a refetch.
  - Called imperatively (Tier 2B): use plain @AuraEnabled, WITHOUT cacheable=true.
    cacheable=true called imperatively returns platform-cached data — a Refresh button
    calling it will appear to do nothing even though it runs.
  - Need a Refresh affordance on @wire data? Keep @wire + cacheable=true and call
    refreshApex(wiredResult) — do NOT switch to imperative just for that.
  - A method that performs ANY DML can NEVER be cacheable=true — a Salesforce platform
    restriction enforced at runtime, not a style preference.

LWC EVENT HANDLER SYNTAX — CRITICAL:
LWC uses "on" prefix for all event handlers. It does NOT support Vue.js or Angular syntax.

  CORRECT (LWC):                         WRONG (Vue/Angular — FORBIDDEN in LWC):
  onclick={handleClick}                  @click={handleClick}
  onchange={handleChange}                (change)={handleChange}
  onkeyup={handleKeyUp}                  v-on:keyup={handleKeyUp}

Button patterns (use names appropriate to the ticket):
  <lightning-button label="{ticketLabel}" onclick={handlerMethod}></lightning-button>
  <lightning-button label="{ticketLabel}" onclick={handlerMethod} disabled={isLoading}></lightning-button>

lightning-card slot placement rule:
  When a ticket asks for a button or action in the card HEADER, use slot="actions" on the element.
  Without slot="actions", any element placed inside lightning-card renders in the card BODY, not the header.
  Slot name reference: "actions" → header right area | (none) → card body | "title" → card title area

Using @click causes compile error: LWC1124 — @click is not a valid attribute.

TIER 2B TEMPLATE DATA ACCESS — CRITICAL:
this.opportunities (Tier 2B) is a plain Array with NO .data/.error — bind
if:true={opportunities}, for:each={opportunities}, if:true={error}, {error}
directly. Using {opportunities.data} causes "Cannot read properties of
undefined (reading 'data')" — that shape belongs to Tier 2A (@wire) only,
never mix the two.

If confused about which pattern to use: re-read the TIER 2 DECISION RULE
above. As a fallback only: default to imperative.

-- JS PATTERN (Tier 2B, imperative Apex DML with toast feedback) --
import { LightningElement, api, track } from 'lwc';
import { ShowToastEvent } from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent';
import updateInvoiceStatus from '@salesforce/apex/InvoiceController.updateInvoiceStatus';

export default class InvoiceStatusUpdater extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;
    @track isLoading = false;

    handleMarkPaid() {
        if (this.isLoading) {
            return;
        }
        this.isLoading = true;
        updateInvoiceStatus({ invoiceId: this.recordId, newStatus: 'Paid' })
            .then(() => {
                this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                    title: 'Success',
                    message: 'Invoice marked as paid.',
                    variant: 'success'
                }));
            })
            .catch((err) => {
                this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                    title: 'Error updating invoice',
                    message: getErrorMessage(err),
                    variant: 'error'
                }));
            })
            .finally(() => {
                this.isLoading = false;
            });
    }
}

-- APEX PATTERN (Tier 2B DML method) --
@AuraEnabled
public static void updateInvoiceStatus(Id invoiceId, String newStatus) {
    if (invoiceId == null || String.isBlank(newStatus)) {
        throw new AuraHandledException('Invoice Id and status are required.');
    }
    update new Invoice__c(Id = invoiceId, Status__c = newStatus);
}

TIER 2B DML RULES:
- Plain @AuraEnabled — never cacheable=true on a method that performs DML.
- Import ShowToastEvent from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent' for feedback
  on both success and failure — never silently succeed or fail.
- Validate required parameters in Apex; throw AuraHandledException with a
  clear message — never let a raw system exception reach the client.
- Use .finally() (or reset in both .then()/.catch()) to clear isLoading
  exactly once regardless of outcome.
- Guard with an isLoading check to prevent double-submission from a
  double-click.

== APEX CONTROLLER FOR LWC ==

-- WHEN TO READ AN EXISTING CONTROLLER --
Check orgState.existingLwcApexController.
IF NOT NULL → the class already exists in the org.
  1. Read the FULL existing body from orgState.existingLwcApexController.body
  2. Add the new method(s) the ticket requires
  3. Emit COMPLETE class — ALL existing methods preserved + new method appended
  4. Emit ApexClass + ApexClassMeta file entries (both required)

-- DECIDING A NEW METHOD'S CACHEABLE-NESS IN MERGE MODE --
orgState.existingLwcApexController.body is raw Apex source text — no separate
structured method/annotation list is provided. When adding a NEW method:
  1. Read each EXISTING method's own @AuraEnabled annotation directly from
     the body text immediately above its signature — never assume every
     method in the class shares one annotation.
  2. Decide the NEW method's cacheable-ness from what THIS ticket needs the
     NEW method to do (apply the TIER 2 DECISION RULE) — never copy a
     neighboring existing method's annotation just because it's in the same
     class. A controller can and often should mix cacheable=true read
     methods with plain @AuraEnabled write methods.
  3. Never change an EXISTING method's annotation unless the ticket
     explicitly asks to change what that method does.

IF NULL → generate the class fresh.
  Naming rule: {ComponentName}Controller with first letter uppercase
  Example: component "invoiceList" → class "InvoiceListController"

-- APEX CONTROLLER TEMPLATE --
public with sharing class InvoiceListController {

    @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
    public static List<Invoice__c> getInvoices(Id accountId) {
        return [
            SELECT Id, Name, Amount__c, Status__c, CreatedDate
            FROM Invoice__c
            WHERE Account__c = :accountId
            ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC
            LIMIT 100
        ];
    }
}

-- APEX FILE ENTRIES — EXACT CONTENT ASSIGNMENT (READ THIS CAREFULLY) --
An Apex class ALWAYS requires exactly 2 file entries. The content types are OPPOSITE to what
you might expect. Swapping them is the single most common LLM mistake with Apex files.

  File 1 → metadataType: "ApexClass"
    filePath:   "classes/ClassName.cls"
    xmlContent: ← APEX SOURCE CODE (public with sharing class ...)
                   MUST start with "public" or "@isTest" or "global"
                   NEVER starts with <?xml
                   NEVER contains <ApexClass>

  File 2 → metadataType: "ApexClassMeta"
    filePath:   "classes/ClassName.cls-meta.xml"
    xmlContent: ← META XML ONLY
                   MUST start with <?xml version
                   MUST contain <ApexClass xmlns=...>
                   NEVER contains Apex code

CORRECT EXAMPLE (copy this pattern exactly):
{
  "metadataType": "ApexClass",
  "memberName": "OpportunityListController",
  "filePath": "classes/OpportunityListController.cls",
  "xmlContent": "public with sharing class OpportunityListController {\n    @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)\n    public static List<Opportunity> getOpenOpportunities(Id accountId) {\n        return [SELECT Id, Name FROM Opportunity WHERE AccountId = :accountId];\n    }\n}"
},
{
  "metadataType": "ApexClassMeta",
  "memberName": "OpportunityListController",
  "filePath": "classes/OpportunityListController.cls-meta.xml",
  "xmlContent": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<ApexClass xmlns=\"http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata\">\n    <apiVersion>61.0</apiVersion>\n    <status>Active</status>\n</ApexClass>"
}

APEX CONTROLLER RULES:
- ALWAYS use "public with sharing" — never "without sharing" unless ticket explicitly requires it
- READ operations (SELECT only): @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
- WRITE operations (DML): @AuraEnabled — no cacheable
- NEVER put SOQL inside loops (governor limit violation)
- ALWAYS use a WHERE clause with a bound variable — never return all records without filter
- Parameters match what the LWC JS calls: getInvoices({ accountId: this.recordId })
- If ticket does not specify a class name → use {ComponentName}Controller

-- WHEN TICKET SAYS THE CLASS ALREADY EXISTS --
If the ticket explicitly says "the class already exists" or "use existing ClassName":
  1. orgState.existingLwcApexController will contain the body
  2. Do NOT regenerate it from scratch
  3. Only add the new method the ticket requests if it is not already there
  4. If the method already exists, do not duplicate it

== COLORED STATUS INDICATORS (BADGES) ==
Use when: the ticket wants a colored badge/label/chip indicating a status,
priority, or category (e.g. "show a colored badge based on Status").

<lightning-badge> (the LWC base component) supports ONLY TWO variant values:
"base" (default grey) and "inverse" — there is no "brand"/"warning"/
"success"/"error" variant on this component, even though those names exist
on OTHER base components (lightning-button, lightning-icon). Salesforce
does not error on an invalid variant value — it silently falls back to the
default grey badge, so every branch of a status-conditional ends up
rendering identically (and wrongly) with no deploy-time signal anything is
broken.

CORRECT — for an actually colored badge, use plain SLDS markup with SLDS
theme utility classes instead of <lightning-badge>:
  <span class="slds-badge slds-theme_success">Closed</span>
  <span class="slds-badge slds-theme_warning">In Progress</span>
  <span class="slds-badge slds-theme_error">New</span>
This needs NO css file and no custom styling — slds-theme_success/warning/
error/info are pre-defined SLDS classes that already carry their own colors.

WRONG (compiles and deploys, but every badge silently renders grey):
  <lightning-badge label="New" variant="brand"></lightning-badge>
  <lightning-badge label="Closed" variant="success"></lightning-badge>

Only reach for <lightning-badge> itself when the ticket wants a plain,
uncolored badge (variant="base") or a badge meant for a dark/inverse
background (variant="inverse") — never pass it any other variant value.

== NEVER ==
- Never use @click, @change, or any @ event syntax — LWC uses onclick, onchange, etc.
- Never pass a variant other than "base" or "inverse" to <lightning-badge> —
  those are the only two values this component supports. For a colored
  status badge, use <span class="slds-badge slds-theme_success/warning/error/info">
  instead — see == COLORED STATUS INDICATORS (BADGES) == above.
- Never use @wire for DML or a user-action-triggered operation — use Tier 2B.
  Never mix data shapes: @wire results are ALWAYS {property}.data/.error;
  Tier 2B results are ALWAYS a plain value with neither. Never assign
  directly to a wired property's .data — call refreshApex() instead.
- Never put <?xml into an ApexClass file — ApexClass.xmlContent MUST be Apex source code starting with "public"
- Never put Apex code (public with sharing...) into an ApexClassMeta file — its xmlContent MUST be meta XML starting with <?xml
- Never put <?xml version into a .html or .js file
- Never put <template> or import statements into a .js-meta.xml file
- Never use document.getElementById or direct DOM manipulation — use template refs
- Never leave off the error handling block in Tier 2
- Never hardcode Salesforce record IDs in JS
- Never skip the lightning-spinner in Tier 2 components
- Never use innerHTML — it is not supported in LWC
- Never forget the key={uniqueField} attribute on for:each items
- Never generate a FlexiPage XML file — the pipeline handles page placement itself.
  When the ticket asks for the component to go on a record page, FlexiPageHelper appends it
  to the existing page in Apex, working from the page's real deployed XML. Emitting your own
  FlexiPage would overwrite that page and destroy every component already on it.
- Never generate a CSS file — out of scope for now
- Component communication (parent-child, events, siblings, LMS): see the
  LWC_COMMUNICATION generation guidance's own == NEVER == additions.
